WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.375
MARK MIRCHANDANI: The public
sector is full of challenges

00:00:02.375 --> 00:00:04.290
when it comes to
adopting new technology.

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:06.510
Join us in historic
downtown Austin, Texas

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:08.820
as we talk to SADA Systems
about these challenges

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:10.546
and how they've built dotMaps.

00:00:10.546 --> 00:00:13.833
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.833 --> 00:00:15.000
Thanks for joining me, Oleg.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.554
Tell me a little
bit more about SADA.

00:00:16.554 --> 00:00:18.170
OLEG SHALYGIN: SADA
is traditionally

00:00:18.170 --> 00:00:20.630
a consulting company
that has migrated

00:00:20.630 --> 00:00:25.970
users from on-prem solutions
to cloud-based platforms

00:00:25.970 --> 00:00:27.500
like Cloud Platform.

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:30.920
Through that experience, we've
gained all this expertise

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:33.110
in various verticals,
specifically

00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:34.700
in the public sector space.

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:37.400
That's where my team
comes in and helps

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:41.897
enable the public sector
leveraging cloud, specifically.

00:00:41.897 --> 00:00:43.980
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So from
a technical perspective,

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:45.725
what makes the public
sector different?

00:00:45.725 --> 00:00:48.770
OLEG SHALYGIN: So the public
sector has unique challenges.

00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:53.240
And traditionally, they've
been under-served by on-prem,

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:56.780
slow-to-roll-out solutions
that really do not scale.

00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:00.350
And we see this very
frequently in all of the RFPs

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:03.590
and all of the requests for
scalability and the ability

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:05.690
to integrate with
various services

00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:07.385
that they already have today.

00:01:07.385 --> 00:01:08.843
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
So what does that

00:01:08.843 --> 00:01:11.162
look like, when they
need to come in and bring

00:01:11.162 --> 00:01:12.620
a new solution to
something they've

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:14.592
been doing for 20 or 30 years?

00:01:14.592 --> 00:01:16.425
What are the technical
challenges with that?

00:01:16.425 --> 00:01:17.750
OLEG SHALYGIN: So the
technical challenges

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:19.970
there are very interesting,
because they have

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:21.560
a system that they use today.

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:24.523
And they say, look, what
you're offering is amazing.

00:01:24.523 --> 00:01:25.940
But for the interim,
we would like

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:27.440
to continue to
use these systems.

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:29.540
And also, we have
various departments

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:31.640
within our organization
that are not going

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:33.420
to start using your system.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:35.990
They're going to continue to
use the application that they're

00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:41.360
familiar with, maybe
ArcGIS, Esri, what have you,

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:45.050
or some database solution,
maybe an Access database.

00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:47.360
They're going to continue
to use that solution.

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:50.600
But we want
[? Atom ?] or dotMaps

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:54.980
to feed that data in, real
time, and integrate seamlessly,

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:57.030
provide rich analytics,
and so forth.

00:01:57.030 --> 00:02:00.830
So they're not ready to
completely abandon the existing

00:02:00.830 --> 00:02:02.810
solutions, but they
want to migrate

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:05.255
and start using
modern technology,

00:02:05.255 --> 00:02:06.380
and the cloud specifically.

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:08.213
MARK MIRCHANDANI: But
it's also a technology

00:02:08.213 --> 00:02:11.120
that's built on top of what they
have, not to replace all of it.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:12.078
OLEG SHALYGIN: Exactly.

00:02:12.078 --> 00:02:15.950
So what we have is, we integrate
with their existing systems,

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:21.320
and we're pulling data almost
real time into the application,

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:23.840
into the cloud,
consuming that data,

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:26.720
and essentially making that
workflow seamless for them.

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:28.430
So they're using
the existing systems

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:31.190
while also leveraging
the new technology

00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:32.538
that our products offer.

00:02:32.538 --> 00:02:34.580
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So let's
talk a little bit more

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:36.630
about dotMaps, for example.

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:37.580
How is dotMaps built?

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:39.747
OLEG SHALYGIN: Yeah, so the
architecture of dot maps

00:02:39.747 --> 00:02:43.190
is essentially living on GKE,
Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:02:43.190 --> 00:02:46.670
And we're using Cloud SQL
for the data storage layer.

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:50.540
And essentially, it's broken up
into various microservices that

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:52.100
handle very specific needs.

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:54.770
So we have a public
portal viewer.

00:02:54.770 --> 00:02:56.310
We have a calendar.

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:58.370
We have all of the
various integrations

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:01.730
that we have within
specific cities.

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:04.100
And all of those are
built and isolated

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:06.380
within their own
services that can then

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:08.587
scale up and down accordingly.

00:03:08.587 --> 00:03:10.670
MARK MIRCHANDANI: And
that's one of the nice parts

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:11.600
about having the cloud.

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:11.900
OLEG SHALYGIN: Exactly.

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:14.210
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So they
are able to use Google Maps

00:03:14.210 --> 00:03:16.400
Platform to do the
actual interface,

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:18.780
while the work gets
done in Kubernetes

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:19.930
and another database.

00:03:19.930 --> 00:03:20.885
And that's about it.

00:03:20.885 --> 00:03:22.430
OLEG SHALYGIN: Exactly.

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:25.010
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So when
considering that architecture,

00:03:25.010 --> 00:03:28.385
has that helped
people start to adopt?

00:03:28.385 --> 00:03:29.520
OLEG SHALYGIN: It has.

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:33.470
So the traditional
user that uses

00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:36.950
dotMaps from the utility
space or the public space--

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:39.020
they're already familiar
with Google Maps.

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:42.360
And they want the experience
to be very, very similar.

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:46.220
So as with Google Maps, nobody
gets trained on Google Maps.

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:49.147
They just log in and,
where do I need to go?

00:03:49.147 --> 00:03:50.480
Where are my points of interest?

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:52.222
What have you--
that's already there.

00:03:52.222 --> 00:03:53.930
And that's the same
experience that we're

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:55.290
trying to push to our users.

00:03:55.290 --> 00:03:57.290
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Well,
thank you so much, Oleg,

00:03:57.290 --> 00:03:59.480
for coming in and telling
us about how SADA built

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:00.800
these solutions in the cloud.

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:01.220
OLEG SHALYGIN: Thank you.

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:02.120
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:03.420
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Thanks for watching,

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:04.920
and stay tuned for
the next episode,

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:07.810
where we dive into the
technical side of [? Atom. ?]

